  "comments": {
    "c1": {
      "anchor": "people#table-1",
      "kind": "table",
      "label": "2. Spaces and people / orgs",
      "excerpt": "Spell today AT Protocol owen@spell , a person An account (a DID, its permanent i",
      "text": "what if an org was just another space, and each member could write to anything in it, subject to permission rules we apply on the writing server?\n\nSo joining an org means you get write access to the org space.  User levels of \"viewer\", \"editor\", \"author\", \"admin\" at the org level, then again at the app level.\n\nIf spell owns the keys to write at the org level, then all writes go through spell, and it can enforce the permission model.  Yes, this means running a server, but it's pretty straightfoward, no?",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T04:55:31.634Z",
      "status": "new",
      "quote": "A dedicated DID that owns spaces, and can change hands without any one person's account",
      "offset": 137,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T04:58:31.559Z"
    },
    "c2": {
      "anchor": "people#table-1",
      "kind": "table",
      "label": "2. Spaces and people / orgs",
      "excerpt": "Spell today AT Protocol owen@spell , a person An account (a DID, its permanent i",
      "text": "There is something powerful about each person having their own \"space\" and the org also having a \"space.\"  \n\nspell.spaces => myspace was a connection my partner got right away and really liked -- she remembers myspace as the first place where you could establish any identity you wanted, and liked that.  We discussed that was back when \"creating things on the web\" was fun because it wasn't so complicated, and you had the freedom to whatever you wanted (with all the downsides of ugly pages that brought).",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T04:58:47.400Z",
      "status": "new",
      "quote": "An account",
      "offset": 46,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T05:00:34.352Z"
    },
    "c3": {
      "anchor": "people#table-1",
      "kind": "table",
      "label": "2. Spaces and people / orgs",
      "excerpt": "Spell today AT Protocol owen@spell , a person An account (a DID, its permanent i",
      "text": "I guess this is literally true in terms of at protocol space, but",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-01T05:01:01.772Z",
      "status": "new",
      "quote": "A Space, named by its authority, type and key",
      "offset": 322,
      "edited": "2026-11-01T05:01:15.888Z"
    }
  },
